      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name blends “Franken” for Franconia and “Mut” meaning courage, so Frankenmuth literally means “courage of the Franconians.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankenmuth%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1904 Frankenmuth became a village, and it was incorporated as a city on October 1, 1959.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankenmuth%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A historic mission church, the first large building in the settlement, was built by the Lutheran group that intended to start a mission among Native Americans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankenmuth%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The settlers arrived in Michigan after a voyage aboard the Caroline, then traveled via canals and Lakes to Saginaw before heading to the hill that would become Frankenmuth.",
